# Cache rules.
#
# The pages here are plain HTML that pull in plain .js and .css by name, with no
# build step and no fingerprint in the filename. That is what makes this site
# easy to work on, and it has one sharp edge: a browser is free to keep an old
# script while fetching the new page that goes with it.
#
# When that happens the page calls a function the stale script has never heard
# of, throws before it finishes starting up, and everything after that line
# never runs. What you see is a half-drawn page where no button works — which
# reads as "the site is broken" rather than "these two files are from different
# days". It happened once; that's enough.
#
# must-revalidate doesn't mean "download it every time". The browser still keeps
# the file and still asks first; when nothing has changed the answer is a 304
# with no body. The cost is one round trip, and what it buys is that the markup
# and the script it depends on can never be from different deploys.

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/*.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Images and fonts don't have this problem — nothing calls a function inside a
# PNG — so they're free to sit in the cache for a week.
/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/*.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/*.webp
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/*.woff2
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
